  "Question": "Which of the following algebraic fractions is in lowest terms?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((x^2-y^2)/(x+y)\\)",
    "B": "\\((x+y)/(x^2+y^2)\\)",
    "C": "\\(2x/(4y)\\)",
    "D": "\\((x-y)/(y-x)\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "In \\((x^2-y^2)/(x+y)\\), the factor \\(x+y\\) can be canceled; in \\(2x/(4y)\\), the factor 2 can be canceled; and \\((x-y)/(y-x)=-1\\) can be reduced. However, \\(x+y\\) and \\(x^2+y^2\\) have no common factor, so \\((x+y)/(x^2+y^2)\\) is in lowest terms.",
